# Airport Context — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

Stage-zero factual packet for the research swarm. Establishes the current
governance, capital, airline, financial, and operating context at the
Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority (MWAA) relevant to a proposed
common acoustic and passenger-communication standard at Washington Dulles
International (IAD) and Ronald Reagan Washington National (DCA). This packet
does not argue the thesis. It sets the shared facts.

## 1. Decision framing (from the run prompt and manifest)

- **Named operator:** Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority (MWAA),
  operator of IAD and DCA under a 50-year federal lease.
- **Scope:** New and substantially renovated terminal spaces at both
  airports.
- **Decision required:** Whether — and in what tiered form — MWAA should adopt
  a common "quiet-by-design" standard covering acoustics, public-address
  zoning, information systems, and operational controls; which requirements
  are mandatory across both airports, which vary by terminal, and how
  compliance is measured.
- **Decision owner (not specified in manifest):** In MWAA's operating model,
  a technical design standard of this type would originate from Engineering
  and Airport Development in coordination with Airport Operations and
  Communications, be issued by the President and CEO through the design
  manual process, and — if it materially affected capital cost, airline
  premises, or tenant obligations — require MWAA Board approval by
  resolution.
- **Time horizon, approval path, success measure, operator context:** All
  fields empty in `outputs/run-manifest.json`. No operator-supplied source
  files, source_material, or source_library entries. This packet is built
  entirely from public, cited sources.

## 2. Governance and delegated authority

- MWAA is an independent public body created by interstate compact between
  Virginia and the District of Columbia; it operates IAD and DCA under a
  federal lease from the U.S. Department of Transportation originally signed
  in 1987 and extended through 2067.
- **17-member Board of Directors.** Appointing bodies: Governor of Virginia,
  Governor of Maryland, Mayor of the District of Columbia, and the President
  of the United States. Members serve six-year terms.
- **2026 Board leadership:** Chair Mark Uncapher; Vice Chair Alex Vogel;
  Secretary Felice C. Smith (re-elected). Trent Morse joined the Board in
  January 2026 for a six-year term.
- **Executive:** John E. "Jack" Potter, President and CEO. Board committees
  material to a design standard: Finance; Business Administration; Strategic
  Development; and the standing Committee of the Whole.
- Board resolutions are the operative instrument for adopting or amending
  major standards, budgets, and the airline use agreement.

## 3. Finance, ratings, and cost-per-enplanement pressure

- Outstanding revenue-bond debt: ~$4.9 billion across 19 series as of MWAA's
  July 2025 disclosures. First-lien pledge on airport system revenues; rate
  covenant of 1.25x debt service coverage.
- Most recent ratings: Fitch and S&P Global (May 21, 2025); Moody's (May 16,
  2025). June 2025 issuance included Aa3/AA- AMT bonds pricing at ~4.37%
  (2036 maturity).
- CPE trajectory: MWAA's 2025 Official Statement projected cost per
  enplanement rising to $20.71 by 2030 — before the July 29, 2026
  announcement of a $20B+ Dulles program.
- Financial documents (Official Statements, ratings reports, CAFR/ACFR, debt
  schedule) are published at mwaa.com Investor Relations.

## 4. Airline use-and-lease framework

- **Current agreement:** 15-year Airport Use Agreement and Premises Lease
  approved by the MWAA Board in 2024, effective **January 1, 2025**, running
  through 2039.
- Required a majority of flights (signatory carriers) to sign before taking
  effect; the agreement addresses the historic revenue imbalance between DCA
  and IAD.
- Bundled with a $9B capital construction program at adoption. That program
  has since been superseded at Dulles by the July 29, 2026 announcement of
  a $20B+ transformation (see §5). The revised program is materially larger
  than the 2024 agreement contemplated; the mechanism for airline consent to
  the larger program is a live financial and governance question.
- Major carriers: **United Airlines** is the dominant hub carrier at IAD (and
  a formal partner in the July 2026 Dulles announcement); **American
  Airlines** is the largest carrier by slot holdings at DCA.
- Any MWAA standard that materially affects airline gate systems, tenant
  paging rights, or airline-controlled hold-room audio would engage the use
  agreement's majority-in-interest and consultation provisions.

## 5. Capital program — the environment a design standard would govern

### 5.1 Dulles (IAD)
- **July 29, 2026:** MWAA, United Airlines, and USDOT announced a **$20B+
  ("more than $22.5B" per press reports) decade-long transformation** of
  IAD. Announced elements: modernization and expansion of existing
  concourses; accelerated AeroTrain expansion enabling retirement of the
  mobile lounges; upgraded baggage handling; expanded CBP federal inspection
  facilities; preservation of the Saarinen main terminal.
- Funding mechanism is not fully disclosed publicly — likely combination of
  MWAA revenue bonds, federal grants, and potential public-private
  participation. Press reporting notes the plan is roughly 3x the $7B
  program the Board adopted in 2024.
- **Tier 2 / Concourse E** (the anchor of the earlier program) remains in
  active construction: 435,000 sq ft, 14 gates, initial phase scheduled to
  open **September 2026**, designed by Turner–PGAL, LEED Silver. Phase 2
  planned to start 2028. HOK selected as designer for future Tier 2 and Tier
  3 concourse expansions, a new federal inspection facility, baggage
  improvements, and a replacement central utility plant.
- May 2026: $41M federal grant awarded for Concourse E construction.
- **New master plan (adopted 2025):** long-range envelope of ~90 million
  annual passengers.

### 5.2 Reagan National (DCA)
- **Project Journey** — the previous major terminal program (new North
  Concourse, secure connector) — is complete. No comparable megaproject
  currently announced.
- Congressionally driven change: the **FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024
  (enacted May 16, 2024)** added 10 slot exemptions (5 daily beyond-perimeter
  round trips) at DCA. In December 2024, DOT awarded one exemption pair each
  to Alaska (San Diego), American (San Antonio), Delta (Seattle), Southwest
  (Las Vegas), and United (San Francisco), with service required by March
  17, 2025. This has added transcontinental peaks, longer dwell times, and
  larger aircraft to a terminal footprint sized for regional operations.
- **January 29, 2025:** the mid-air collision between PSA Airlines Flight
  5342 (CRJ-700) and an Army Black Hawk on approach to DCA led to
  helicopter-route restrictions and heightened operational stress that
  persisted into 2026 planning.
- Any DCA-specific quiet-by-design requirement must contend with the
  Historic District status of Terminal 1 (the original 1941 Bolling
  building) and the architectural intent of the César Pelli Terminal 2, both
  of which limit the acoustic treatments and PA hardware retrofits available
  on the existing footprint.

## 6. Passenger traffic (calendar 2025)

- **IAD:** all-time record — **~29.0M total passengers**, **10.5M
  international**. Growth continued into 2026.
- **DCA:** ~24.9M total passengers, down 5.3% from 26.3M in 2024. Lowest
  count since 2022. Drivers cited: the January 2025 mid-air collision and
  ensuing route restrictions; a seven-week federal government shutdown in
  fall 2025; softer government-related travel demand.
- Implication for a standard: DCA operates near its physical envelope with
  falling traffic; IAD is growing rapidly against a much longer capital
  runway. A single MWAA standard will meet two very different demand
  contexts.

## 7. Regulatory, safety, and accessibility dependencies

- **FAA:** 14 CFR Part 139 airport certification; AC 150/5220-16E (airport
  terminal building) and related advisory circulars set minimum design
  guidance; FAA also administers AIP and BIL grants.
- **TSA:** checkpoint layouts, queue design, and public-area announcement
  content (security announcements) fall under TSA authority.
- **CBP:** federal inspection facility standards are relevant to the IAD
  program.
- **Emergency communication:** NFPA 72 (Chapter 24, Emergency Communications
  Systems) governs mass-notification and voice-evacuation intelligibility
  (STI/CIS thresholds); IBC/IFC adopted by Virginia and DC apply.
- **Accessibility:** ADA Title II and the DOT Air Carrier Access Act; ACAA
  amendments (2024 rulemaking) on airline accessible communication;
  ACRP-published human-factors research on multimodal wayfinding. A
  quiet-by-design standard that reduces PA output shifts more communicative
  burden onto visual, digital, and staffed channels — all of which have
  their own accessibility rules.
- **OSHA:** occupational noise exposure standards apply to employees; a
  standard that specifies ambient sound and speech-intelligibility targets
  interacts with employee-exposure obligations at gates, ramp offices, and
  checkpoints.

## 8. Public commitments and constraints most relevant to this run

- The 15-year use agreement (2025–2039) is the binding commercial envelope
  in which any new design standard lives.
- The July 29, 2026 Dulles announcement pushes the practical decision window
  forward: design standards baked into program-level briefs before schematic
  design (60–90% pricing) will be materially cheaper than retrofits.
- Concourse E opening in September 2026 is the first live test bed at IAD
  for whatever standard MWAA adopts; DCA's existing terminals are the
  brownfield test.
- No public MWAA acoustic standard, PA-zoning standard, or announcement
  governance policy was located in this stage-zero pass. Absence of a
  published standard is itself the operative fact for this run.

